mory), then when `lookup.get_value()` reads `u.format` (a HBUINT16 / BEInt<unsigned short, 2>), it would be reading from invalid memory.\n\nBUT - the sanitize function validates the substitution table lookups. So the question is: can we craft a font where sanitize passes but runtime still reads OOB?\n\nActually, I think I found the issue. Let me re-read the sanitize code:\n\nThe `substitutionTables` field is:\n```cpp\nLOffsetTo<UnsizedOffsetListOf<Lookup<GlyphID>, HBUINT32>, false>\n    substitutionTables;\n```\n\nThe `false` template parameter means \"not nullable\" (or \"do not check for null\"). \n\nAnd `substitutionTables.sanitize(c, this, num_lookups)` sanitizes the list with `num_lookups` entries.\n\nBut wait, the `UnsizedOffsetListOf::sanitize` calls `UnsizedOffsetArrayOf::sanitize(c, count, this)` which calls the parent `UnsizedArrayOf::sanitize(c, count, base)`. This checks each offset.\n\nLet me look at the regression range: \"201810160143:201810160344\". This is from October 2018.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be more subtle. Let me look at what happens with a format that has a lookup. The `LookupFormat0::sanitize` uses `c->get_num_glyphs()` to validate the array. But during the apply phase, `LookupFormat0::get_value(glyph_id, num_glyphs)` checks `if (unlikely (glyph_id >= num_glyphs)) return nullptr;` - this is fine.\n\nFor format 8, `LookupFormat8::get_value`:\n```cpp\nreturn firstGlyph <= glyph_id && glyph_id - firstGlyph < glyphCount ?\n       &valueArrayZ[glyph_id - firstGlyph] : nullptr;\n```\n\nAnd the sanitize checks the valueArrayZ has glyphCount entries. So this should be OK too.\n\nWait, let me look at the `UnsizedOffsetListOf::operator[]` more carefully:\n```cpp\ninline const Type& operator [] (unsigned int i) const\n{\n    return this+this->arrayZ[i];\n}\n```\n\nThere's NO bounds checking on `i` here! So if `entry->data.markIndex` is, say, 5, but during sanitize only 3 lookups were validated (because the entries traversed during saniti