nitized? Let me think...\n\nActually, I think there might be a bug where the version/format of the StateTable changes interpretation. Let me look at what type of StateTable is expected.\n\nWait - I just realized something. Let me re-read the crash more carefully:\n\n\"BEInt<unsigned short, 2>::operator unsigned short\" from \"AAT::ContextualSubtable::driver_context_t::transition\"\n\nThe BEInt read is 2 bytes. But the crash is \"READ 1\" - meaning 1 byte overflow. So it's reading 2 bytes and only the last byte is out of bounds. This strongly suggests the Lookup format field read is the culprit - it's at the very end of the blob with only 1 byte available.\n\nBut I established that Lookup::sanitize checks `u.format` which is 2 bytes, so if only 1 byte is available, sanitize would fail on `c->check_struct(this)`.\n\nUNLESS the sanitize check passes because of some alignment/boundary issue, or because there's a different code path.\n\nWait - let me re-read ContextualSubtable::sanitize:\n\n```cpp\ninline bool sanitize (hb_sanitize_context_t *c) const\n{\n    TRACE_SANITIZE (this);\n    if (!machine.sanitize (c))\n        return_trace (false);\n    \n    unsigned int num_entries = 0;\n    // ... sweep to find entries ...\n    \n    unsigned int num_lookups = 0;\n    // ... scan entries for markIndex/currentIndex ...\n    \n    return_trace (substitutionTables.sanitize (c, this, num_lookups));\n}\n```\n\nWait, I need to look at exactly what `substitutionTables.sanitize(c, this, num_lookups)` does. `substitutionTables` is an `LOffsetTo<UnsizedOffsetListOf<Lookup<GlyphID>, HBUINT32>>`, which is an `OffsetTo<..., HBUINT32>`.\n\n`OffsetTo::sanitize(c, base, args...)`:\n```cpp\ntemplate <typename T, typename OffsetType=HBUINT16, bool has_null=true>\nstruct OffsetTo : Offset<OffsetType, has_null>\n{\n    ...\n    inline bool sanitize (hb_sanitize_context_t *c, const void *base, Ts... ds) const\n    {\n        TRACE_SANITIZE (this);\n        if (unlikely (!c->check_struct (this)